  "question": {
    "text": "A heating element has a resistance of <math><mn>100</mn><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math> at room temperature. When it is connected to a supply of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>220</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>, a steady current of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> passes in it and temperature is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>500</mn><msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>o</mi></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> more than the room temperature. The temperature coefficient of resistance of the heating element is"
